<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chand dynasty</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Chand dynasty ruled the hill kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumaon">Kumaon</a>, in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttarakhand">Uttarakhand</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, until the Gorkha conquest of 1790. Tradition traces it to Som Chand, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> prince from the plains who settled at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/champawat">Champawat</a>, though its early chronology is legendary. By the 15th and 16th centuries the Chands had subdued rival hill chiefs, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalyan-chand">Kalyan Chand</a> moved the capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almora">Almora</a> around 1563.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chand-dynasty.jpg" alt="Chand dynasty" width="320" height="205" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hyder Young Hearsey (1782-1840) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fort_and_the_capital_city_of_Kali_Kumaon,_Champawat,_1815.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The traditional genealogical lists of the Chand kings, the vamshavalis, were compiled in the mid-19th century and are not completely reliable. They place Som Chand in the 7th century, and a British report of 1849 accordingly dated the start of Chand rule to 685 CE. Historians dispute this, because the Katyuri kingdom is known to have ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumaon">Kumaon</a> until the third quarter of the 10th century.</p><p>Tradition makes Som Chand an immigrant from Jhusi and a relative of the king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannauj">Kannauj</a>. The historian Krishna Pal Singh suggests that he came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumaon">Kumaon</a> during the upheaval of the Ghaznavid invasion of Kannauj in 1018–1019, and dates the beginning of Chand rule to 1019–1021. By the early 13th century the Chands appear to have ruled as feudatories: the Baleshwar Temple inscription of 1223, set up by Krachalla Deva, a conqueror from present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>, lists men whose names suggest that they belonged to the family.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Rudra Chand (1565–1597) seized Sira in 1581 and made the first of several attacks on the Garhwal Kingdom, all of which were repulsed. In 1587 he visited the Mughal emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a> as a mark of obeisance, and the Ain-e-Akbari lists a Sarkar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumaon">Kumaon</a>. Baz Bahadur Chand (1638–78), one of the most powerful of the line, joined Shahjahan in an attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garhwal">Garhwal</a> in 1655 that captured the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/terai">Terai</a> region including Dehradun, and extended his territory east to the Karnali river. In 1672 he began a poll tax whose revenue was sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> as tribute.</p><p>In 1744 the Rohilla leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-mohammed-khan">Ali Mohammed Khan</a> sent a force that took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almora">Almora</a> against weak resistance. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rohillas">Rohillas</a> stayed seven months and left after a payment of three lakh <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rupee">rupees</a>; a second attack was defeated at Barakheri. Hill troops under Dip Chand later fought beside the Rohillas at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-battle-of-panipat">Third Battle of Panipat</a> in 1761. Internal strife over the following thirty years cost the kings most of the land they had held in the plains.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gurkhas">Gurkhas</a> invaded early in 1790, crossing the Kali River, and the Chands were driven to the Bhabhar and finally expelled. Nepalese rule lasted twenty-four years. During the Anglo-Nepalese War Harak Deo Joshi, minister of the last Chand raja, sided with the British, who captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almora">Almora</a> on 26 April 1815; with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-sugauli">Treaty of Sugauli</a>, ratified on 4 March 1816, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumaon">Kumaon</a> passed to British rule. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/champawat">Champawat</a>, the first capital, keeps a medieval fort and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balasore">Baleshwar</a> and Nagnath temples, and Chand kings also built temples at Bageshwar, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhimtal">Bhimtal</a> and Almora.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chand dynasty” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>According to tradition the <b>Chand dynasty</b> was founded by Som Chand, a Rajput prince from the plains, who established himself at Champawat in eastern Kumaon.</blockquote><a href="/state/kumaon-kingdom">Kumaon Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
